# Every environment variable production needs, with no real values in it.
#
# This file is documentation, not a deployment artifact: in production these are set as the Coolify
# compose resource's environment variables, not as a committed .env. Copy it to an UNCOMMITTED
# `.env.prod` when you want to render or dry-run compose.prod.yml locally:
#
#   cp docs/deploy/production.env.example .env.prod      # .env.prod is gitignored; keep it that way
#   docker compose -f compose.prod.yml --env-file .env.prod config
#
# Conventions worth knowing before you fill it in:
#
#   * ASP.NET reads `__` as the section separator, so `ConnectionStrings__TakeDB` is
#     `ConnectionStrings:TakeDB`. compose.prod.yml maps the names below onto those; production uses
#     `__` throughout because a `:` in a variable name upsets some .env parsers and web UIs.
#   * ASPNETCORE_ENVIRONMENT is NOT in this list and must stay unset. Unset means Production, which
#     is what turns off Swagger, skips appsettings.development.json, and makes the auth cookie
#     Secure. Setting it to anything is how you accidentally ship Swagger.
#   * Mark everything flagged SECRET below as secret in Coolify, and leave "Is build variable?" OFF
#     for all of them. Nothing in this deployment builds.
#   * Substitute your own domain for `example.com` throughout. Keep the API on a SUBDOMAIN of the
#     web app's domain — that is what lets COOKIE_DOMAIN work with the cookie's default
#     SameSite=Lax. A genuinely different domain needs SameSite=None, which is a code change.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Images. Set where: NOT in Coolify any more — see below.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

# These two no longer apply to the deployment. Coolify runs two Docker Image Applications now, and
# `.github/workflows/deploy.yml` pins each one's image digest through the Coolify API
# (docker_registry_image_name/_tag) rather than through a compose variable. So the image a
# deployment runs is not an environment variable at all, and a rollback is a workflow_dispatch of
# `Deploy` with `pin_tag` — see docs/deploy/operations.md section 2.
#
# That is a real improvement rather than a shuffle: a pin here was a value somebody had to remember
# to REMOVE, and one left behind would silently hold production on an old image through every
# subsequent release. `pin_tag` applies to one run.
#
# They are still live for `compose.prod.yml` run LOCALLY, which is what that file is for now —
# pulling one exact published artefact to smoke test it before cutting a release:
# API_IMAGE=ghcr.io/pi-gorbo/takeinitiative-api@sha256:<64 hex>
# WEB_IMAGE=ghcr.io/pi-gorbo/takeinitiative-web@sha256:<64 hex>

# Only if you mirror the pinned MinIO rebuild into GHCR (the command is in compose.prod.yml).
# Unset, the digest-pinned docker.io reference in compose.prod.yml is used.
# MINIO_IMAGE=ghcr.io/pi-gorbo/minio:RELEASE.2026-08-04T00-00-00Z@sha256:<64 hex>

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Hostnames and origins. Not secret. Set where: Coolify env.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

# The web app's public origin, scheme included. Becomes CORS__MainApp, CORS__AdminApp,
# TakeUrls__Web (the link in confirmation and password-reset emails) and NUXT_PUBLIC_WEB_URL.
WEB_ORIGIN=https://takeinitiative.example.com

# The API's public origin, scheme included. Becomes NUXT_PUBLIC_AXIOS_BASE_URL, which the browser
# reads out of window.__NUXT__.config at request time — so changing it is a redeploy, not a rebuild.
API_ORIGIN=https://api.takeinitiative.example.com

# The API's host NAME only — no scheme, no port. Becomes AllowedHosts, and therefore also the Host
# header the api healthcheck presents to itself: /healthz is host-filtered and answers 400 to
# anything not named here. Semicolon-separate if there is genuinely more than one name; the
# healthcheck uses the first.
API_HOST=api.takeinitiative.example.com

# The cookie's domain. The LEADING DOT is what makes one auth cookie valid on both hostnames above.
COOKIE_DOMAIN=.takeinitiative.example.com

# The sending domain for outbound email; becomes no-reply@<this>. Must be a domain verified with
# SendGrid, or sends fail (sign-up still works — PostSignUp logs the failure and signs the user in).
EMAIL_DOMAIN=takeinitiative.example.com

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Database. SECRET. Set where: Coolify env, from the Coolify-managed Postgres 15 resource.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Postgres is NOT a service in compose.prod.yml: it is a Coolify-managed database resource, so its
# lifecycle is independent of app redeploys and it gets Coolify's scheduled backups. Pin it to 15.
# <pg-host> is the internal hostname Coolify shows on that resource; reaching it needs "Connect to
# predefined network" enabled on the compose resource. It has no published port, by design.
# SECRET
TAKEDB_CONNECTION=Host=<pg-host>;Port=5432;Database=takeinitiative;User ID=takeinitiative;Password=CHANGE_ME;

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The object store. SECRET. Set where: Coolify env.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

# One pair of credentials, used twice: MinIO's root user/password AND the API's Blobs__AccessKey /
# Blobs__SecretKey. compose.prod.yml wires both from these, so they cannot drift — but if you
# replace MinIO with a managed bucket, these become that provider's key pair instead.
# MinIO refuses to start with a user under 3 characters or a password under 8.
# SECRET
BLOBS_ACCESS_KEY=CHANGE_ME
# SECRET
BLOBS_SECRET_KEY=CHANGE_ME

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Email and keys. SECRET. Set where: Coolify env.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

# SendGrid API key, from a free account with a verified sender on EMAIL_DOMAIN.
# SECRET
SENDGRID_API_KEY=CHANGE_ME

# Bound to JWTOptions, `required` there, and never actually read — a leftover from the pre-cookie
# auth. Set 64 random characters anyway so startup does not fail, and expect nothing from it.
# SECRET
JWT_SIGNING_KEY=CHANGE_ME

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Not in this file, and why
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Fixed in compose.prod.yml, because they are properties of the deployment rather than of the
# environment, and an operator getting one wrong is a silent failure:
#   Blobs__Bucket=takeinitiative          Blobs__Region=us-east-1
#   Blobs__ServiceUrl — http://minio:9000 LOCALLY, but MinIO's own Coolify resource hostname in the
#   deployment, now that it is a separate resource. The one entry in this list that is per environment
#   Blobs__ForcePathStyle=true            Blobs__CreateBucket=true
#   Marten__ApplySchemaOnStartup=true     DataProtection__KeyPath=/keys
#
# Baked into the web image: HOST, PORT, NODE_ENV. Left unset deliberately:
# NUXT_PUBLIC_SUGGESTIONS_BASE_URL (the model weights ship in the image and are served from its own
# origin) and ASPNETCORE_ENVIRONMENT (see the header).
#
# GitHub Actions secrets, not container variables — set on the repository, not here. The full
# one-time setup is docs/deploy/pipeline.md section 5:
#   COOLIFY_BASE_URL     repository VARIABLE (not a secret), with the https:// scheme. A variable
#                        because masking a hostname turns every failure into *** — a lesson paid for
#   COOLIFY_API_TOKEN    `prod` Environment secret. read + write + deploy; not root
#   TS_OAUTH_CLIENT_ID   `prod` Environment secret. The Tailscale client that mints
#                        tag:ci-takeinitiative-prod
#   TS_AUDIENCE          `prod` Environment secret. The workload-identity-federation audience
# Superseded, and safe to delete once the first pinned deploy has worked: COOLIFY_DEPLOY_URL and
# COOLIFY_TOKEN, which drove the old webhook that could only re-pull a moving tag.
# There is deliberately NO registry secret: GITHUB_TOKEN covers the push, the packages are public so
# nothing on the VPS needs credentials to pull, and the pipeline resolves digests with an anonymous
# ghcr.io pull token.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Consumers outside the deployment
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# The knowledge-base ingest CLI (apps/TakeInitiative.KnowledgeBase.Cli, step 26) is a consumer of
# this deployment, not part of it. It runs from the OPERATOR'S OWN MACHINE against the production
# database over an SSH tunnel, because Postgres has no published port — which is why it is here as a
# documented reader of TAKEDB_CONNECTION rather than as a service. It reads
# KnowledgeBase:ConnectionString (env `KnowledgeBase__ConnectionString`, flag `--connection`), which
# is the same connection string as TAKEDB_CONNECTION with the host and port rewritten to the local
# end of the tunnel. The API owns the schema, so the API must have started against that database at
# least once before an ingest can write to it, and `--dry-run` goes first.
# The two commands, and where the tunnel's local port comes from, are docs/deploy/operations.md (29h).
